      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Pro-inflammatory cytokines may play an important role in patient response to cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB). Since the myocardium is proposed to be a major source of cytokines, we studied the influence of the cardiolpegia type on interleukin-6 release and early myocardial recovery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN",
          "content": "prospective, randomized study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "university hospital, operative and intensive care."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "20 consecutive patients (3 females) scheduled for elective coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG), mean age 62.8+/-5 years, history of myocardial infarction 11/20, left ventricular ejection fraction 62.9+/-15%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "patients were operated on using randomly either cold blood cardioplegia (B, n = 10) or cold crystalloid cardioplegia (C, n = 10)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASURES",
          "content": "plasma levels of interleukin-6 (IL-6) were measured prior to CPB, after aortic declamping, after CPB, 1 hour, 6 hours and 12 hours postoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Groups were comparable with respect to demographic data, left ventricular function, number of grafts, CPB and aortic crossclamp time. Group B patients demonstrated significant lower IL-6 levels after 1 hour (210+/-108 vs. 578+/-443 pg/ml), 6 hours (204+/-91 vs. 1210+/-671 pg/ml) and 12 hours (174+/-97 vs. 971+/-623 pg/ml). Post-CPB cardiac index was superior in group B (3.9+/-0.3 vs. 3.2+/-0.3 l/min/m2, p<0.05) with similar doses of inotropes. Group B patients could earlier be weaned off respirator (10+/-4 vs. 13+/-4 hours, p<0.05) and showed minor blood loss (635+/-211 vs. 918+/-347 ml, p<0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Inflammatory response to CPB is associated with delayed myocardial recovery. The use of blood cardioplegia may attenuate inflammatory reactions."
        }
      ]},
